<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aden Emergency</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> Emergency was an insurgency against British rule in Aden and the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, lasting from 1963 to 1967. It began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a>'s uprising in the Radfan mountains on 14 October 1963 and grew into urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare in Aden, fought by the NLF and the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-liberation-of-occupied-south-yemen">FLOSY</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> withdrew on 30 November 1967, handing power to the NLF, which proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>'s independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aden-emergency.jpg" alt="Aden Emergency" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_soldiers_in_gas_masks,_Aden_emergency.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The British East India Company landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-marines">Royal Marines</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> on 19 January 1839, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Lahej was forced to cede it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> built a web of protection treaties with the sheikhdoms around it. Aden became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown Colony</a> in 1937. On 18 January 1963 the colony was merged into the new Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, which Aden's commercial elite opposed. The coup of 26 September 1962 that created the Yemen Arab Republic, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, inspired the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-liberation-of-occupied-south-yemen">Front for the Liberation of Occupied South Yemen</a>, which drew support from the Radfan countryside and from Aden respectively.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared a state of emergency on 10 December 1963 after an NLF grenade attack on the High Commissioner, Sir Kennedy Trevaskis, at Khormaksar Airport killed his adviser and a woman and injured fifty people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> went on to kill off-duty British officers and policemen, much of the violence falling in Crater, the old Arab quarter. The rebel groups also fought one another, and their own losses were far higher than Britain's. In January 1967 the NLF provoked street riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, and an Aden Airways Douglas DC-3 was bombed in mid-air with everyone on board killed.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had helped Israel, and on 20 June 1967 hundreds of soldiers of the federal army mutinied, joined by the Aden Armed Police; the mutineers killed 22 British soldiers and occupied Crater. In July the 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> and Sutherland Highlanders under the command of Colin Campbell Mitchell retook the district overnight without casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Renewed NLF attacks led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to leave by the end of November 1967, earlier than Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a> had planned and with no agreement on who would govern, and the People's Republic of Southern Yemen was proclaimed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-liberation-of-occupied-south-yemen">FLOSY</a>, without Egyptian support, lost to the NLF and the federal army and disbanded. Britain lost 90 to 92 soldiers killed and 510 wounded, with 17 British civilians dead; local government forces lost 17 killed, and the rebels 382 killed and 1,714 wounded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aden Emergency” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 14 October 1963 the National Liberation Front (NLF) launched an armed uprising in the Radfan mountains, beginning the <b>Aden Emergency</b>, a guerrilla and urban war against British forces and the federal government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
